WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.013 --> 00:00:05.536
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.536 --> 00:00:08.939
[BLANK AUDIO] Hello and

00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:13.892
welcome to our webinar with the BBSA.

00:00:13.892 --> 00:00:18.166
My name is Famatta Mansa and
I'm a second year here at the GSB, and

00:00:18.166 --> 00:00:22.620
I'm joined by two of our incoming
presidents of the BBSA, as well.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.280
I'm joined by Elise and Ruth.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.099
Like me,
they're also current students, and

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:32.414
as I mentioned their incoming
presidents for the BBSA.

00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:36.051
We'll be talking about our
personal journey to the GSB,

00:00:36.051 --> 00:00:40.443
some of our career decisions, and
then life after the GSB for me, and

00:00:40.443 --> 00:00:44.170
answer as many questions
as we can during this time.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:48.130
To submit a question all you have
to do is click the Q&amp;A button

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.250
at the bottom of your screen.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.690
We won't be talking about the admissions
process or financial aid.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.712
If you do have those questions we
encourage you to reach out to admissions

00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:01.960
and to financial aid to get those
specific questions answered.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.660
Let's begin with introductions.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.510
Elise and Ruth, can you please start by
telling us a little bit about yourselves.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:15.206
Where you're from, what you'll be doing,
what you were doing before you came

00:01:15.206 --> 00:01:18.587
to the GSB, and
what you'll be doing after graduation.

00:01:18.587 --> 00:01:19.801
Elise, let's start with you.

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:21.100
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks, Famatta.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.210
Hi, everyone, I'm Elise Smith.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.380
I am originally from Chicago.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.425
As Famatta mentioned, I am an MBA1.

00:01:30.425 --> 00:01:34.620
I'm also a joint degree student in
pursuing a Masters in education.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.030
Prior to getting to Stanford I was at
IBM Watson where I was developing early

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.970
iterations of Watson for
education products, and

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.620
leading ed tech partnerships.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.136
And more recently was at New Schools
Venture Fund, a philanthropic fund that

00:01:47.136 --> 00:01:50.670
invests in the education space working
in their Diverse Leaders portfolio.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.589
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:01:53.589 --> 00:01:54.413
Ruth,how about yourself?

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:57.948
&gt;&gt; Hi everybody, my name is Ruth,
originally from Nigeria,

00:01:57.948 --> 00:02:01.141
I'm an MBA1 incoming
president like Famatta said.

00:02:01.141 --> 00:02:04.253
I started my career at Ernst &amp; Young where
I audited several entertainment, and

00:02:04.253 --> 00:02:06.815
healthcare, and
real estate companies in Los Angeles.

00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:10.978
But have also spent a significant portion
of my career working in different

00:02:10.978 --> 00:02:14.613
capacities on the African continent,
particularly in Kenya and

00:02:14.613 --> 00:02:16.411
Nigeria in fintech companies.

00:02:16.411 --> 00:02:19.423
So I'm super excited to be here and
to answer you guys' questions.

00:02:19.423 --> 00:02:20.528
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you guys.

00:02:20.528 --> 00:02:23.900
Let's get started with some
questions submitted by the audience.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.312
Well, this one's a pretty easy one.

00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:30.656
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Why did you come to Stanford, and

00:02:30.656 --> 00:02:32.455
what did you come to learn?

00:02:35.789 --> 00:02:36.567
&gt;&gt; Do you want to start?

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:39.235
&gt;&gt; Sure, so why did I come to Stanford?

00:02:39.235 --> 00:02:40.910
I came to Stanford because I got in,
number 1.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.821
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:02:41.821 --> 00:02:46.325
&gt;&gt; But I'd always known that I wanted
to get my MBA, because I knew I wanted

00:02:46.325 --> 00:02:50.550
to pivot my career from more finance
focused to more tech focused.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.860
Like I said in my introductions,
I am from Nigeria.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.030
And so one of my life goals is to help

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.200
bring African-owned products
to the rest of the world.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.490
And I felt that Stanford was
the best place for that.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:07.180
Not just in terms of getting your MBA,
like you can at other schools, but

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.950
really a hub for creativity like I've
never seen before in any other MBA campus.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.640
And then what was the second question?

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.810
&gt;&gt; What did you come to learn?

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:15.730
I think you-
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:16.912
that's exactly what I came to learn.

00:03:16.912 --> 00:03:19.170
And to just meet amazing people
from all over the world,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.260
which has definitely been
a check off the check list.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, similarly coming to Stanford for

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.855
me was because it offered something
that no other school did.

00:03:28.855 --> 00:03:33.251
And so the first, it's the Masters in
education joint degree program with

00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:37.992
the MBA, you can do it in two years, and
which is a really positive experience and

00:03:37.992 --> 00:03:39.139
part of why I came.

00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:42.540
But also I knew I was interested
in social entrepreneurship.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.474
I knew that I wanted to be in
an area where ed reform and

00:03:45.474 --> 00:03:49.226
social justice were part of kind
of the fabric of the environment.

00:03:49.226 --> 00:03:52.490
And I think that, paired with kind
of the entrepreneurial spirit,

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:56.330
the VC spirit here in the Bay was
something that I couldn't turn down.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think I definitely
resonate with you guys

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.850
on just the global experience.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:02.970
For me it also was a lot
of the soft skills,

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.180
the ability to hone in and
invest in myself.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.812
So I think that's also a really unique
thing that Stanford does bring,

00:04:11.812 --> 00:04:15.212
whether that's through
interpersonal dynamics,

00:04:15.212 --> 00:04:20.618
also known as touchy-feely, whether
that's coaching and developing others and

00:04:20.618 --> 00:04:25.118
some of our peers, I think those
are two important things, as well.

00:04:25.118 --> 00:04:28.788
What made you, I assume Ruth,
you're a pretty successful person,

00:04:28.788 --> 00:04:32.664
that you got into some other schools,
as well, and same as you, Elise.

00:04:32.664 --> 00:04:37.306
I think you guys talked about it a little
bit, but can we really double down on why

00:04:37.306 --> 00:04:42.459
did you choose Stanford, potentially over
other opportunities that you may have had?

00:04:42.459 --> 00:04:46.076
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think it would double
touch on what you mentioned, Famatta,

00:04:46.076 --> 00:04:50.063
and that I think the Stanford program
really focuses on becoming a leader that

00:04:50.063 --> 00:04:54.171
others want to follow, that helps you take
time to think about what that means for

00:04:54.171 --> 00:04:55.643
your leadership style, and

00:04:55.643 --> 00:04:59.801
then gives you a bunch of opportunities
to practice that and live through that.

00:04:59.801 --> 00:05:03.585
And so whether it's giving a low keynote
where you get to think about what's

00:05:03.585 --> 00:05:06.127
a point of view or
perspective you want to share, and

00:05:06.127 --> 00:05:08.905
then getting to give a Ted-like
talk to the community,

00:05:08.905 --> 00:05:13.085
I took advantage of that opportunity
this year, that was really powerful.

00:05:13.085 --> 00:05:16.675
Whether it's taking touchy-feely, getting
to be a coach and a mentor for other

00:05:16.675 --> 00:05:21.131
students, or going through leadership
labs where that's your first quarter,

00:05:21.131 --> 00:05:24.840
you're working with a team and kind of
a squad, is what they call them, and

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.592
getting to get feedback about how you come
off, giving feedback to kind of your peers

00:05:29.592 --> 00:05:32.820
in this really exciting kind
of collaborative environment.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:36.636
I didn't see that in other programs
as much as an emphasis, and

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:39.156
that was something that was important for

00:05:39.156 --> 00:05:42.830
me knowing that I'd want to lead
my own venture in the future.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:43.717
&gt;&gt; I think for me,

00:05:43.717 --> 00:05:47.967
funny enough, I chose Stanford because
I was out of my comfort zone here.

00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:52.835
I'm used to, I used to do audits, so the
process, everything is really laid out for

00:05:52.835 --> 00:05:56.346
you and it's up to you to really
follow through on the rules.

00:05:56.346 --> 00:05:59.823
And I really felt like said here your
experience is really what you make it

00:05:59.823 --> 00:06:03.642
because administration really wants to
see people do as many different types of

00:06:03.642 --> 00:06:04.970
things as possible.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:09.260
And I think at other schools, number 1, it
might be easy to get lost in the numbers

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.215
and then you're just kind of following
the process that's laid out for you,

00:06:12.215 --> 00:06:16.655
but here it's really about, yes you can do
things like lead labs and low keynotes.

00:06:16.655 --> 00:06:20.955
But you can also be fully working on your
company and getting funded for that and

00:06:20.955 --> 00:06:24.425
be one of many students getting funded for
really amazing things that,

00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:26.810
like they're doing, I know Elise is one
of those students getting funded for

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:27.800
an amazing company.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.940
So for me knowing that the school also
focused more on the individual and

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.780
less on clusters, really spoke
a lot to me, because I felt like

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.720
I might experience would be different
then all the other 400 students.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.400
And I really felt strongly
that I would get that year,

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.245
also you can't beat California's weather.

00:06:46.245 --> 00:06:49.061
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And so,

00:06:49.061 --> 00:06:51.655
all of those things are the reason
why I chose Stanford.

00:06:51.655 --> 00:06:56.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think Stanford has so
many niche opportunities, too.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:01.472
I think for us, there's so many students
doing different things, from some of

00:07:01.472 --> 00:07:06.467
my classmates who are bee keepers and
working in agricultural, there's just so

00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:11.401
many things that people are doing, and
especially things I never knew before.

00:07:11.401 --> 00:07:12.032
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely.

00:07:12.032 --> 00:07:16.016
&gt;&gt; Everyone is kind of doing their own
thing, but able to utilize the resources

00:07:16.016 --> 00:07:20.105
to their advantage, as you guys talked
about, and as you definitely do now.

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:23.280
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Working on your own amazing venture,

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.530
and I think that's really
unique about Stanford,

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:30.390
is you have all the resources of the
broader university, while still being able

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:35.159
to be part of this organization and
move forward with everyone else.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:37.088
So speaking of,

00:07:37.088 --> 00:07:40.810
from your guys' perspective what
is the Stanford community like?

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:45.049
If someone were to ask you,
what makes Stanford Stanford?

00:07:45.049 --> 00:07:48.010
Talk about kind of the cultural
element and what you've experienced.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.290
Yeah, I think I would say one of
the things I love is this focus on kind of

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:57.310
vulnerability and being open and
being your authentic self.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:01.500
It's something that I think is really
highlighted in the program Talk where

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.440
students get to talk about,
45 minutes to share what ever they want.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.294
Often times its personal
stories about their lives or

00:08:08.294 --> 00:08:10.716
things that they have
thought about deeply.

00:08:10.716 --> 00:08:14.643
And the whole community comes out and
BA ones and BA two's, and I think that,

00:08:14.643 --> 00:08:17.908
people are just standing giving
an ovation for ] I don't know,

00:08:17.908 --> 00:08:19.920
like five minutes it seems sometimes.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.860
And I think that to me is such a hallmark
of the community, and I think it comes

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.710
out then in other places in just
one-on-one conversations and coffee chats.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.690
Someone got a coffee chat
with me the other day,

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.020
we were talking about media and new media.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.470
But we didn't get there until
like the last 15 minutes.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.340
And for the first 30 minutes,
it was just us learning about each other

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.590
getting to know what drives us talking
about having a partner at the GSB.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:47.830
And so I think that to me, the piece where
it's like you're not necessarily this

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:50.980
business person where you're like, I'm
going to get a coffee chat to learn about

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.010
finance, and
I'm going to only talk about that.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.260
It's like we're people first and

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.750
I think that's something that's
really beautiful about the community.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:57.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:02.093
Something that I love about Stanford
community is actually just the layout

00:09:02.093 --> 00:09:03.181
of the community.

00:09:03.181 --> 00:09:07.041
So for those who haven't visited
Stanford yet, we have classes, really,

00:09:07.041 --> 00:09:11.160
on one side of the street, and residential
areas on the other side of the street.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.610
So it takes all of two minutes to
get from your doorstep to class.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.395
And it's not even just about
the distance it takes to get to class,

00:09:18.395 --> 00:09:22.470
it's more about the community
that you have as a first year

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:26.710
where you're not driving extremely
far to see your classmates.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:30.010
Or if you're going through something,
whether it's a homework assignment or

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.320
something at home,
either your roommates or

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:37.488
400 friends are there with you,
within a really small radius.

00:09:37.488 --> 00:09:43.860
So over 95% of, I would say,
first years live in these residences.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:44.660
They're pretty nice.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:48.406
It does feel like a golf resort a lot of
the days, but I also think that it helps

00:09:48.406 --> 00:09:52.960
to create a real, true sense of community
because you're not worried about distance.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.495
We do, so many events in the residences,

00:09:55.495 --> 00:10:00.150
from birthdays to bridle
showers to you name it.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:01.080
We just hangout together.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.210
We also definitely have
time to go to the city, but

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:08.120
I think that that part really just creates
a natural space for people to get to know

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:11.540
each other outside of what might be
a natural click in another area.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.805
So, for example, none of my friend
groups are a status quo group and

00:10:14.805 --> 00:10:18.870
I really like that and I think that
the residences really help to create that.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.186
Yeah, but
even if you don't live in the residences,

00:10:21.186 --> 00:10:23.613
[LAUGH] you still you can still
be a part of the community.

00:10:23.613 --> 00:10:25.106
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, true, true, true.

00:10:25.106 --> 00:10:25.912
&gt;&gt; Very true.

00:10:25.912 --> 00:10:28.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, great.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:29.480
We have some more questions.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.030
So I think this is a great one for us.

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:36.020
So getting a little bit into more
of the pre application process.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:42.500
Have any of you been involved in MLT or
other programs.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:43.950
These school applications.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:47.940
And we're laughing because
[LAUGH] MLT was what I did.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.380
Ruth, I know you did it.

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:50.205
&gt;&gt; I did it, yeah.

00:10:50.205 --> 00:10:51.626
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:10:51.626 --> 00:10:54.349
why don't we take a second
to talk about that.

00:10:54.349 --> 00:10:57.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for me personally,
MLT was a life changer.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:02.519
Coming from LA I didn't really
have a circle of people that were

00:11:02.519 --> 00:11:05.740
like minded and ambitious as I was.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:10.470
And I think the vibe that MLT
provides you with just like amazing

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.890
people of color from all over the country.

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:19.920
I think almost 300 were in our cohort
who are as ambitious as you are.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:23.600
That really helped me in terms of
this whole application process.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:27.820
I also wasa weird case where
I did MLT from Africa.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:32.600
So it was still relatively easy for
me to keep

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:37.590
in contact with my coach
as well as my MLT cohort.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:41.056
So in terms of MLT really being just like
you feel like you're being in the trenches

00:11:41.056 --> 00:11:42.312
together with other people,

00:11:42.312 --> 00:11:45.797
you getting recommendations together,
you're reading each other's essays.

00:11:45.797 --> 00:11:49.057
I know one of my classmates helped
me read my essay for Stanford.

00:11:49.057 --> 00:11:51.115
We read each other's essay and
we both got in.

00:11:51.115 --> 00:11:52.927
So MLT was really amazing.

00:11:52.927 --> 00:11:56.389
I know there are other programs like For
Women and things like that, but

00:11:56.389 --> 00:11:59.642
those things really helped me feel like
I had a community even before I got to

00:11:59.642 --> 00:12:02.540
Stanford and
I still have that community today.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:03.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:08.040
I think for me MLT was really helpful and
valuable, but I think because

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.240
I wasn't confident that I wanted to go to
business school and it was still something

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:14.810
I was weighing, I think I maybe got
a little bit less out of the program.

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:20.220
I also I wasn't necessarily interested
in going to just any business school,

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.180
and I think for
me I really had my heart set on Stanford.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.760
And so I think, although it was really
helpful to go through that kind of program

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:31.120
and explore a bunch of different programs,
I think for me I knew I wanted to

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.060
come here, and I wasn't sure if
business school was the right thing.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.748
Camp was coming most recently from
the social sector and so you think it was

00:12:40.748 --> 00:12:45.738
a great network and a great experience but
I think without it it also is possible

00:12:45.738 --> 00:12:50.222
to apply and get to school and
make a network in community here as well.

00:12:50.222 --> 00:12:53.106
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:53.106 --> 00:12:57.051
So to clarify what MLT is-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:57.051 --> 00:12:58.720
&gt;&gt; We should do that first.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.800
It is management leadership for tomorrow.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:06.816
It's essentially a program that's
geared at filling the pipeline

00:13:06.816 --> 00:13:11.891
from the C suite,
all the way down to entry into college.

00:13:11.891 --> 00:13:17.240
And this specific program is the MBA prep.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:21.470
So that's one of the lines,
the pipelines in there.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:25.970
So what MLT specifically does
is geared at African Americans,

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:29.130
Hispanics, and Native Americans, again,

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:34.620
to fill the pipeline of traditionally
underrepresented minorities in the space.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:36.610
And so it's just a coaching program.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.130
You have a cohort as Ruth mentioned.

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:40.750
It's really just a support system for

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.070
about a year before
business school starts.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:47.670
So really understanding the process,
knowing what it takes to get in,

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:51.940
knowing what your profile needs to be,
and you get coaching, and so

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:55.830
it's, essentially, a great program,
and highly recommended.

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:58.160
Feel free to look it up.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:02.250
I also did MLT, and for me, I think,
similar to Ruth, what she mentioned,

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:05.500
is my cohort was very instrumental
in even me coming to Stanford.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:12.520
I already knew a ton of people who had
gotten in round one, I applied round two.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:17.440
So it was great just having people who
I knew look like me, who I had met,

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:22.853
who look extraordinary people but also
people that I've shared a drink with or

00:14:22.853 --> 00:14:28.042
gone to lunch with, actually having
someone that I knew went to Stanford.

00:14:28.042 --> 00:14:31.600
I think prior to MLT, I didn't know
any anybody that went to Stanford.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:35.280
So it just made it still aspirational, but

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:39.750
an achievable concept for me.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:43.211
Let's take on applications.

00:14:43.211 --> 00:14:44.935
I love this question.

00:14:44.935 --> 00:14:48.550
Were you ever scared at any
point in time of applying?

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.342
And is the school just full of nerds?

00:14:51.342 --> 00:14:55.161
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Question, definitely not.

00:14:55.161 --> 00:14:57.180
&gt;&gt; I don't think I'm a nerd,
I don't know about you guys.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:58.170
&gt;&gt; I mean, is being a nerd a bad thing?

00:14:58.170 --> 00:14:59.220
&gt;&gt; I aspire to be a nerd.

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:02.190
&gt;&gt; It is Nerd Nation, so-

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:03.940
&gt;&gt; Nerd Nation, you want to-

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:05.016
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure,

00:15:05.016 --> 00:15:07.980
I think scared is an interesting word.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.049
I think for me,

00:15:09.049 --> 00:15:13.835
it always felt like you couldn't
guarantee you were going to get in.

00:15:13.835 --> 00:15:15.088
&gt;&gt; M-hm.
And I think that's true for

00:15:15.088 --> 00:15:15.980
every business school.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:19.140
And I think perhaps especially true for
[LAUGH] Stanford.

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:23.290
And so I think for me it was always,
one, is business school right for

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:27.320
me, especially where I'm at now in my
journey, is this the best step to take?

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:33.362
And then, two,
it's such a time investment to apply.

00:15:33.362 --> 00:15:36.608
Almost it felt like a different
job outside of my regular job.

00:15:36.608 --> 00:15:40.300
And had to get support from mentors and
advisers, and

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.120
it was like, I'm going to go through this.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:45.070
I want to make sure I'm putting
my time in the right place.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.220
And so I think it was scary in that sense.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:52.010
It was scary to Invest so much time into
something that didn't feel for sure.

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:57.190
And then also I wasn't sure, and so
a lot of those uncertainties were at play.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:15:59.710
Are we all nerds?

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.892
I don't know how to answer that.

00:16:01.892 --> 00:16:05.910
[LAUGH] I hopefully would
aspire to be a nerd.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:09.520
I enjoy the classes a lot and love
learning, and if that makes me a nerd,

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:13.860
then I guess there I am.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:17.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; For me, I wasn't scared because

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:21.180
I really was applying to
Stanford blind in a lot of ways.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:25.190
Similar to Femetta I didn't really
know people that went to Stanford.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:29.500
I knew one person that went to
Stanford before I got in and

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:34.130
I know that there people who might have
known dozens of people just based on

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:35.690
people that worked with them.

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:37.980
Or friends or family members and
things like that.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:42.430
So I don't even think I knew enough to
be scared, to be honest with you guys.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:46.910
But I think that I really just applied
on my best foot, and I think we

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:50.800
might get into some questions about when
is it right to apply and things like that.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:54.900
But I applied knowing that this
is the best I have to offer, and

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.380
thankfully it was good enough.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:00.170
So super, super happy about that.

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:03.460
In terms of being a nerd here,
are we all nerds?

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:07.330
I actually think what I have noticed
at Stanford is that people really

00:17:07.330 --> 00:17:12.695
aspire to improve
themselves intellectually.

00:17:12.695 --> 00:17:18.275
And that has become currency one of the
different currencies and I actually don't

00:17:18.275 --> 00:17:22.517
think that that's a bad thing because in
terms of having fun like fun is in you.

00:17:22.517 --> 00:17:27.140
So if you can have fun and you're not
a nerd that could be possible, too.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:28.130
So I don't think that,

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:32.460
I think that being a nerd might be
actually a good thing, personally.

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:35.900
But that doesn't take away from
having an amazingly fun and

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:37.210
amazingly great experience.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:38.490
So, that's how I see it.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.330
[LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's well said.

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:44.250
I think exactly what you said,
people here have done amazing things,

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:46.720
are inspiring to do amazing things.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:49.280
I consider people aspirational but

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:55.420
that also sometimes is correlated with
wanting to read a book and [LAUGH].

00:17:55.420 --> 00:18:00.410
But equally we have a lot
of fun I think if you want

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:04.060
to be social there is
something every single day.

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:06.880
I think I've gotten myself
caught into that sometimes.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:08.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Sometimes I have to just fall back and

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:13.840
read a book and watch some Netflix on
the weekend just to calm down, but

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:18.580
I think the school is, you'll find
over 400 students in one grade.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:23.900
So once you kind of combine all
the programs, you have over 800 people.

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:25.070
Just naturally 800 people,

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:26.930
you're going to have a range-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:28.950
&gt;&gt; Of what people do want to do and
what they don't want to do.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:32.720
So I actually consider even the diversity
in people's experiences and

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:33.310
what they want to do.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:36.420
And even on the nerd scale
I say there's a diversity.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:38.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for
those that do want to come and nerd out.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:39.930
There's definitely opportunities.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:40.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; To do that.

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:42.560
You can be super focused on academics.

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:46.310
You could nerd out in terms of one of my
favorite groups is a political group.

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:49.450
Where you come together and
discuss politics once a month.

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:51.830
This past month was super heated.

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:53.040
Some months it's a little more chill.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:53.848
But that thing,

00:18:53.848 --> 00:18:58.070
that's a community that I consider we all
nerd out on different facts like that.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So different ways to be nerd and

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.110
different ways to engage in the community.

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:06.100
&gt;&gt; This might be a good time to ask or
to tell people kind of yours

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:09.520
your favorite activities I think
you mentioned that or clubs.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.505
I can start one of my favorite
clubs is the wine club.

00:19:12.505 --> 00:19:14.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just because it's so

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:18.050
amazing we have Somalias
speaking a little bit of nerdy.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:19.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But in the wine sense.

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:23.410
They come, they do tastings.

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:26.190
We talk about the notes, and
the tenants and all that.

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:30.160
And we've had, especially with
NAPA Valley being right up the road,

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:32.720
we've had special guests come and
special wineries come.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:34.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And teach us and

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:36.620
be a little bit more
educated in the wine space.

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:37.719
So that's one of my favorites.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:40.420
&gt;&gt; There I like so many favorites.

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:41.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; This is really hard.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:42.450
&gt;&gt; Pick two.

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:43.380
Pick one or two.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.300
&gt;&gt; I'm sure we'll talk
about the BBS later.

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:46.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So we can put that aside.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:50.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for now [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I love the social entrepreneurship

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:51.040
community.

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:53.640
So social innovation, there's a club.

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.060
There's the Center for Social Innovation.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:59.300
There's just like a ton of pieces and
ways to plug into that community.

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:03.470
And they've not only been super helpful
and starting something on my own, but also

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:09.080
in just providing support and guidance and
just folks to talk about different things.

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:11.540
And so social entrepreneurship
kind of runs the gamut and

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:13.200
social innovation runs the gamut.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:16.840
But getting to learn from other MBA
students experience has been super

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:19.400
valuable to me,
as well as the advisers and

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:23.730
the mentors and venture studio you can
go work on your idea there at least.

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:26.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I should stop talking because I could

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:27.760
go on forever [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.095
&gt;&gt; I think one of my favorite activities

00:20:30.095 --> 00:20:32.316
here is travelling [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:32.316 --> 00:20:33.242
&gt;&gt; So I'm going to go there.

00:20:33.242 --> 00:20:34.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.092
&gt;&gt; So and the funny thing is at the GSV

00:20:36.092 --> 00:20:38.420
travelling can mean a ton of things.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:41.140
So it can mean going to yellow stone and

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:46.080
spending a weekend hiking and working and
with people that you gotten to know.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:48.830
Or it can be getting an academic
experience in another country,

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:50.680
which we call GSC's here.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:55.120
Or it can be going to Indonesia and
making your own cultural and

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:57.110
academic experience or doing that in Cuba.

00:20:57.110 --> 00:21:01.860
So I love how just very
textured our traveling

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:05.920
experiences are here, you can have
a great time where you do nothing or

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:11.040
you have a lot of alcohol or you can
have a really great learning experience.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:12.760
And it's not just because
the school mandates it,

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:15.430
it's because people have
this drive to really

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:19.100
immerse themselves in different
cultures and in different experiences.

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.860
And humbly get to know those cultures and
experiences.

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:26.650
So in terms of me,
all the places I've been to with my

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:31.580
Stanford whether friends or classmates,
for the purposes of school or for

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:37.140
the purpose of just like us planning
a trip, I've had just amazing time.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:40.840
And even in the times that are not
specifically learning experiences,

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:43.755
I've still learned something because I got
to know my classmates a little bit better

00:21:43.755 --> 00:21:46.450
because we were in
the middle of Cuba together.

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:50.060
Or I got to know what business
is like in Brazil and

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:53.180
what the future of tech in Brazil is
going to look like in the next 10 years.

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:56.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think the point about

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.000
the international component to,
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:01.280
&gt;&gt; We do have a big international
presence here at Stanford.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:06.160
And so that also helps to, you get to
go to your classmates home country.

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:07.577
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; In Mexico.

00:22:07.577 --> 00:22:09.387
&gt;&gt; Kazakhstan.

00:22:09.387 --> 00:22:13.718
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah and so Mexico and
then even some South Korea,

00:22:13.718 --> 00:22:14.907
&gt;&gt; Lebanon.

00:22:14.907 --> 00:22:16.096
&gt;&gt; Lebanon.

00:22:16.096 --> 00:22:17.344
&gt;&gt; Israel, just all over.

00:22:17.344 --> 00:22:19.492
&gt;&gt; Yeah, all over South Africa.

00:22:19.492 --> 00:22:20.169
&gt;&gt; Zimbabwe.

00:22:20.169 --> 00:22:21.015
&gt;&gt; Zimbabwe.

00:22:21.015 --> 00:22:25.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
that's also a really cool experience too.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:28.820
There doesn't have to be an official
school sponsored event but

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:31.920
through your classmates you can still
get that experience and exposure.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:37.240
And those have been some of the best
experiences here at Stanford for me.

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:40.740
So another question is, Stanford
has an exceptionally diverse class.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:42.170
Look at that transition.

00:22:42.170 --> 00:22:46.210
How is it being a person of color at
the TSB considering the environment of

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:50.380
other top schools and low percentages
in the minority communities.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:59.810
&gt;&gt; For me, I would say that being
a minority here, in terms of me

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:03.820
having the identity of being African but
also considering myself black American.

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:07.460
I think that it is different
from other schools

00:23:08.830 --> 00:23:11.390
that have much larger classes,
just because of numbers.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:16.180
So if you have a 400 student class, versus
a 900 student class, obviously there would

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:21.620
be more, or hopefully [LAUGH] I would say,
there would be more people of color.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:22.160
&gt;&gt; From a numbers perspective.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:23.320
&gt;&gt; From a numbers perspective.

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:28.167
But what I love about this
community is that because we

00:23:28.167 --> 00:23:31.980
are smaller we have so many more
opportunities to get to know each other.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.770
And in a lot of ways we feel like
we're in the trenches together

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:39.810
in terms of continuing to create more
access for those coming after us.

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:44.560
I will say that it makes you feel like you

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:48.720
are more of an agent of change rather
than more of a cog in the process.

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:53.150
I don't know if that's the right
phrase and I also love the fact that,

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:56.902
In terms of, I'll speak within
the black community, there's so

00:23:56.902 --> 00:24:00.030
many different experiences
within that community.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:04.438
I've never met a diverse group of black
people like I've met at Stanford.

00:24:04.438 --> 00:24:06.266
And I love that.

00:24:06.266 --> 00:24:09.722
Because I get to see what life is
like if you are from the Midwest or

00:24:09.722 --> 00:24:13.306
what life is like if you're from
the Upper East Side of New York, or

00:24:13.306 --> 00:24:16.784
what life is like if you're from
Johannesburg, South Africa.

00:24:16.784 --> 00:24:19.800
And people get to understand
what my life was like, too.

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:20.683
So I love that.

00:24:20.683 --> 00:24:24.958
In terms of now looking outward for
the rest of our classmates,

00:24:24.958 --> 00:24:29.556
I will say that I've also never met
a more set of intrigued people who

00:24:29.556 --> 00:24:33.770
genuinely want to know what my
experience is as a black woman.

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:37.619
And who want to come into that
with humility as well as just

00:24:37.619 --> 00:24:40.826
a sense of wanting to learn
what my life is like,

00:24:40.826 --> 00:24:44.740
and hopefully wanting to
connect with me in that way.

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:48.440
And so that's also something that I've
seen in a lot of my classmates and

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:52.010
I appreciate from a lot of
my classmates as well, yeah.

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:55.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think,
kind of echoing what you're saying, Ruth.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:59.565
Coming to the GSB and being a woman
of color, being a black woman,

00:24:59.565 --> 00:25:03.412
I think is going to be colored by
your experiences prior to that.

00:25:03.412 --> 00:25:03.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:07.288
&gt;&gt; So I went to a predominantly
white undergraduate institution,

00:25:07.288 --> 00:25:11.507
have operated in a business world which
isn't as diverse as I would like it to be.

00:25:11.507 --> 00:25:15.601
And I think coming to Stanford was
no different than those experiences.

00:25:15.601 --> 00:25:20.800
And if anything, I found the community
to be focused on increasing diversity,

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:22.680
equity and inclusion here.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:26.697
So I'm on the diversity committee, it's
a hugely diverse group of students who

00:25:26.697 --> 00:25:30.418
are like we're out here just pushing
even more advancement in terms of what

00:25:30.418 --> 00:25:32.920
experiences could be like for
students of color.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:35.317
For students of historically
underrepresented backgrounds,

00:25:35.317 --> 00:25:38.436
for students who are in different minority
groups or whole different identities.

00:25:38.436 --> 00:25:40.765
And so to me,
that's kind of part of my way of one,

00:25:40.765 --> 00:25:44.290
doing something that I care deeply about,
something that affects me.

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:46.480
It's also related to the social
venture I'm working on.

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:49.940
And so getting to work in a bunch of
different ways on that has been really

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:50.810
powerful.

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:52.380
And working with the administration,

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:56.810
working with other student leaders on
campus has been really fun and rewarding.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:01.161
I think personally l would echo the black
community at Stanford's super diverse.

00:26:01.161 --> 00:26:03.216
And I think the whole
community is diverse.

00:26:03.216 --> 00:26:06.109
And I think I already mentioned talk.

00:26:06.109 --> 00:26:08.994
But one of the things I loved
about that is it exposes,

00:26:08.994 --> 00:26:11.881
even though this person might
appear a certain way and

00:26:11.881 --> 00:26:16.690
you might assume different identities or
experiences to them and prescribe that.

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:19.670
I think what is amazing about
Stanford is people will surprise you,

00:26:19.670 --> 00:26:22.240
what they've been through,
what they've been, what they've overcome.

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:24.270
And that's true across all backgrounds.

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:28.976
And so I think in many ways,
I feel very much one of so

00:26:28.976 --> 00:26:33.692
many different stories in
this larger community.

00:26:33.692 --> 00:26:36.912
And I think my experience is
one that people seek out and

00:26:36.912 --> 00:26:40.629
I seek out learning about others,
too, so it's pretty good.

00:26:40.629 --> 00:26:45.053
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I would also add that I
think being [COUGH] president of the BBSA

00:26:45.053 --> 00:26:47.540
also provides a unique opportunity.

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:49.719
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So whether that's working with

00:26:49.719 --> 00:26:54.173
administration on our diversity
initiatives, whether that was someone

00:26:54.173 --> 00:26:58.645
tapping me on the shoulder to do some
moderating work yesterday evening.

00:26:58.645 --> 00:27:03.255
I think we are in a very visible position,

00:27:03.255 --> 00:27:06.300
both pun intended and not.

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:08.738
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that allows us to get involved and

00:27:08.738 --> 00:27:11.706
be part of conversations, and
have a seat at the table.

00:27:11.706 --> 00:27:15.760
So I think that's important,
too, [COUGH] all right.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:20.130
So I think speaking of inclusion, this
is I think a really important question.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:24.916
So someone mentioned they really
want to apply to the MBA program, but

00:27:24.916 --> 00:27:28.143
the cost of the program
is a really big hurdle.

00:27:28.143 --> 00:27:30.526
Was that also a concern for
either of you, and

00:27:30.526 --> 00:27:33.620
does Stanford offer any financial support?

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:37.690
First, before they jump in,
I will say, I encourage

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:42.590
you to go to our financial aid on our
website, there's some information there.

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:47.680
And financial aid, as I'm sure this person
asked, is a very personal situation.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:52.860
So take what we say with
the right lens for yourself.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:56.300
But if you do reach out to financial aid,
they will give you more specifics.

00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:59.637
But if you guys don't mind
sharing kind of personally for

00:27:59.637 --> 00:28:03.020
you, Have you thought about it?

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:07.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think definitely the cost
of attending business school is one of

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:10.340
the hurdles for me to get over mentally.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:14.210
And not only applying, but once getting
in, if business school was right for me.

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:19.590
It's a super hard decision, and like
Fometa said, it's super personal as well.

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:25.974
What I can say is that since coming to
the GSB, I felt like I'm getting what

00:28:25.974 --> 00:28:31.003
I'm paying for, [LAUGH] in many ways and
more than I could have imagined.

00:28:31.003 --> 00:28:35.039
And I think starting this venture,
meeting these people,

00:28:35.039 --> 00:28:39.721
getting to do these new experiences
that are so much more kind of scope

00:28:39.721 --> 00:28:45.340
expanding in terms of how I saw my my life
when I saw what is possible for myself.

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:50.278
What careers existed, so what could
be has been such a valuable part of

00:28:50.278 --> 00:28:56.070
the experience that I can't necessarily
put a tangible, this cost that amount.

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:58.510
And therefore it's
a return on my investment.

00:28:58.510 --> 00:29:02.962
But I can say that that's
something you're going to have to

00:29:02.962 --> 00:29:05.846
decide individually for yourself.

00:29:05.846 --> 00:29:10.233
But I have found my time here to be so
valuable already.

00:29:10.233 --> 00:29:15.636
And we're only at kind of the beginning
of our spring quarter first year,

00:29:15.636 --> 00:29:20.531
at least Ruth and I, and so
happy to talk more about that one on one.

00:29:20.531 --> 00:29:24.093
But I think it's something you're going to
have to think deeply about for yourself.

00:29:24.093 --> 00:29:24.713
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:24.713 --> 00:29:29.611
&gt;&gt; I think for me too,
I tend to be very risk averse.

00:29:29.611 --> 00:29:35.580
So I was thinking about how would I
pay this off and things like that.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:39.524
But I think, even as a risk averse person,

00:29:39.524 --> 00:29:45.128
it was very clear to see that
the ROI here was going to be high.

00:29:45.128 --> 00:29:47.130
I think for me, again,

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:52.749
everybody is going to have a different
financially, sort of offer.

00:29:52.749 --> 00:29:57.287
If and when you do get into a school based
on different scholarships that the school

00:29:57.287 --> 00:29:59.079
offers, and things like that.

00:29:59.079 --> 00:30:02.042
But I will say that, even coming here and

00:30:02.042 --> 00:30:07.360
just expanding my mind as to what my
financial future can even look like,

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:11.982
based on my different classmates'
experiences and what I've

00:30:11.982 --> 00:30:17.068
learned from different professors
has made the cost seem so myopic.

00:30:17.068 --> 00:30:20.548
It makes me feel I have thought so
myopically about things whereas I'm like,

00:30:20.548 --> 00:30:24.258
actually this is how I should envision
what my financial future should look like.

00:30:24.258 --> 00:30:26.940
And this is how small this
cost is compared to that.

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:29.234
So I would even encourage
people not to necessarily,

00:30:29.234 --> 00:30:33.930
regardless of what school you're applying,
not to necessarily see this thing as a,

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:36.830
okay, once I graduate school I'm going to
have to be paying off my loans for

00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:39.050
the next two years because,
or the next five years or

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:41.860
ten years because,
I'm probably going to be making this much.

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:47.110
Definitely really just understand
that your financial future

00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:52.763
really is way more expensive than
probably most people think it is.

00:30:52.763 --> 00:30:57.082
And this can be a really small cost if you
really take advantage of really a lot of

00:30:57.082 --> 00:31:01.205
opportunities that you have just because
you would have the Stanford brand

00:31:01.205 --> 00:31:05.606
on your name, throughout your career,
literally for the rest of your career.

00:31:05.606 --> 00:31:09.527
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I will add that for folks who
are thinking about going into nonprofit or

00:31:09.527 --> 00:31:12.603
social sector any sort of perhaps,
less lucrative career,

00:31:12.603 --> 00:31:15.830
there is a loan forgiveness
program that you should look into.

00:31:15.830 --> 00:31:18.858
It's something that was a huge
part of my deciding to come,

00:31:18.858 --> 00:31:22.432
knowing that if I wanted to go back and
work at an education nonprofit or

00:31:22.432 --> 00:31:26.150
do something in that space,
the cost actually is perhaps prohibitive.

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:29.940
But with loan forgiveness, it became a lot
more reasonable and feasible for me,

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:31.065
at least personally.

00:31:31.065 --> 00:31:32.712
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And the school also offers,

00:31:32.712 --> 00:31:36.641
to Lisa's point, opportunities for people
who want to go into the social sector

00:31:36.641 --> 00:31:40.137
right after school, offers different
sorts of fellowship packages.

00:31:40.137 --> 00:31:41.704
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Which again, like Fometa said,

00:31:41.704 --> 00:31:43.548
you guys can look online for
it, but yeah, great point.

00:31:43.548 --> 00:31:44.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:47.835
&gt;&gt; So I'm not going to respond to that
question because there's a flurry

00:31:47.835 --> 00:31:49.511
of [LAUGH] questions going on.

00:31:49.511 --> 00:31:53.160
So I'm going to try to get to all these
questions before we're out of time.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:55.655
So Lisa, I'm going to direct this to you.

00:31:55.655 --> 00:31:56.950
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:31:56.950 --> 00:31:59.480
So, as someone coming
from the social sector,

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:02.210
how have you viewed
the business school experience?

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:06.250
And then for someone who is
interested in the social sector,

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:08.950
what do you say is the value
of business school?

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:10.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:14.135
So I think coming directly from K12
education, which is extremely progressive,

00:32:14.135 --> 00:32:17.502
I was a little taken aback getting
into the the business school realm and

00:32:17.502 --> 00:32:20.308
having people say things like
fiduciary responsibility,

00:32:20.308 --> 00:32:21.729
and I was like, but the kids!

00:32:21.729 --> 00:32:23.903
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I think it has,

00:32:23.903 --> 00:32:25.240
what about the kids.

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:27.870
But I think it has been super valuable.

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:30.140
I think there's classes
like financial modeling,

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:32.700
which are just skills that
are important to know.

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:35.410
Accounting was a struggle
at the beginning, but

00:32:35.410 --> 00:32:38.010
I think it is important
to build that kind of

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:41.540
business acumen whether you're in
the social sector or the private sector.

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:45.910
I think having that joint degree and

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:49.263
being in Masters in Education is also
a nice pairing personally for me.

00:32:49.263 --> 00:32:53.920
In one of my ed classes we were
sitting on the floor, legs crossed,

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:56.010
there was a fire on the projector.

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:56.550
You know what I mean?

00:32:56.550 --> 00:33:00.040
So different vibes, but I also think that
vibe can be found in the business school.

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:03.480
And so choosing classes like
social ventures practicum or

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:07.410
philanthropy, there's just a ton of
that sort of energy in the community.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:10.690
And so
I think although you might come here and

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:14.000
be like everyone's going to be doing
consulting in finance, that's not true,

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:17.760
and in fact so many people talk about
the impact they want to have, and

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:19.810
what that means in whatever
career they're pursuing.

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:21.505
So, yeah.

00:33:21.505 --> 00:33:22.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:23.210
I definitely agree with that.

00:33:23.210 --> 00:33:26.380
I think the other thing too that's really
unique about Stanford that you alluded to

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:29.760
is that there's so
many social related classes.

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:31.991
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; And then I know one of my good friends

00:33:31.991 --> 00:33:36.622
is also a dual in the education program,
and he said that one of those classes on

00:33:36.622 --> 00:33:41.537
just the thought process of instituting
education reform, and then coupling that

00:33:41.537 --> 00:33:46.060
with policy in the business school was
a way that he even thought about that.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:49.250
So I'd say get creative too on how you
think about you think about the social

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:51.410
sector, and what skills on both sides.

00:33:51.410 --> 00:33:53.796
So the private and the public sector,
what you can do there.

00:33:53.796 --> 00:33:59.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; All right, [COUGH] let's move on.

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:05.082
So I think related on mentality and mind
set, Ruth, I think this is a good one too.

00:34:05.082 --> 00:34:09.222
How has the MBA program transformed
your perspective on the issues you

00:34:09.222 --> 00:34:14.064
seek to tackle as a leader in your field,
and have your interactions with other MBA

00:34:14.064 --> 00:34:18.652
students made you consider collaborating
across fields in the near future?

00:34:18.652 --> 00:34:19.413
Yeah.

00:34:22.293 --> 00:34:27.004
&gt;&gt; I think for me in terms of
the type of leader that I want to be,

00:34:27.004 --> 00:34:32.379
I think coming from a very corporate
role before the MBA made me think

00:34:32.379 --> 00:34:37.968
of a leader as kind of, I had this
profile of what a leader looked like.

00:34:37.968 --> 00:34:41.964
And you had to say certain things and
you had to act a certain way and

00:34:41.964 --> 00:34:46.685
you couldn't be vulnerable in an ABC or
D way, whereas here we're literally

00:34:46.685 --> 00:34:50.316
turning the definition of what
a leader is upside down, and

00:34:50.316 --> 00:34:53.260
vulnerability is shown
as a sign of strength.

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:58.220
And being able to be honest about
what type of impact you want to have

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:00.960
is not a bad thing, and
it's not just about the bottom line.

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:04.590
I don't even think I've ever heard that
actually used in terms of what people

00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:10.010
would consider a successful run of any
sort of business or initiative, and so for

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:13.760
me, knowing what I want to do and knowing
the type of impact that I want to have in

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:20.620
Africa, I think that what I've learned
is that it doesn't have to be one way.

00:35:20.620 --> 00:35:24.365
The awesome thing is, for example,
I'm taking a class right now in our design

00:35:24.365 --> 00:35:28.508
school, which is an awesome school, by the
way, called design thinking studio, and

00:35:28.508 --> 00:35:32.537
I'm seeing how the problems that I want to
solve even don't have to be through tech,

00:35:32.537 --> 00:35:35.757
which a lot of people would be
surprised about coming to Stanford.

00:35:35.757 --> 00:35:38.881
They probably think that we're super
tech focused and it's only tech,

00:35:38.881 --> 00:35:39.850
everything is tech.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:43.751
But we're really breaking things
down to the bare minimum,

00:35:43.751 --> 00:35:46.656
literally using our hands
to build things and

00:35:46.656 --> 00:35:51.261
figure out how can we solve a world
problem from so many different ways.

00:35:51.261 --> 00:35:53.984
In my class alone I'm working with
a student from the medical school,

00:35:53.984 --> 00:35:56.950
somebody from school of education,
somebody from the law school, right.

00:35:56.950 --> 00:35:59.640
We're all working on one project together.

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:02.608
And so, I'm learning different
things from how do I solve this

00:36:02.608 --> 00:36:06.309
problem through policy, how do I solve
it through journalism, [LAUGH] right?

00:36:06.309 --> 00:36:10.215
There's so many different ways to
think about solving problems and so

00:36:10.215 --> 00:36:15.046
many different ways of what a leader looks
like that it's helped me to really, again,

00:36:15.046 --> 00:36:19.347
break the stereotypes of the path I
thought I had to go, and really understand

00:36:19.347 --> 00:36:23.850
that there's so many different innovative
ways to solve different problems and

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:25.967
there's so many ways to do that as me,

00:36:25.967 --> 00:36:29.530
not as a profile of somebody
who I think people want to see.

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:30.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.910
I will actually take
this similar question.

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:37.860
But to build on that, I think it's also
important for me, I'm realizing as I'm

00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:42.130
heading out, is that leadership
is a journey, not a destination.

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:45.390
And so as we have classes
like leadership perspective,

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:49.660
we have prominent people coming in and
it's just a they give an intro and

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.000
then they just stop and
we just get to ask them questions.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:55.200
And thinking about chairmans of Microsoft-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:56.268
&gt;&gt; And all these other places.

00:36:56.268 --> 00:37:00.568
Just the ability to ask questions
like how do you manage your career,

00:37:00.568 --> 00:37:02.582
how do you manage a dual career?

00:37:02.582 --> 00:37:03.601
It's been amazing.

00:37:03.601 --> 00:37:07.411
And so really recognizing that even people
at the top of the mountain are still

00:37:07.411 --> 00:37:09.529
working on the next thing for themselves.

00:37:09.529 --> 00:37:10.161
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:10.161 --> 00:37:13.415
&gt;&gt; The similar question here is people
of color face unique challenges in

00:37:13.415 --> 00:37:14.615
the workforce.

00:37:14.615 --> 00:37:17.845
Would you say that GSB gave you the tools
to navigate the unique challenges people

00:37:17.845 --> 00:37:20.550
of color face in the modern workplace?

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:21.670
So I'll take that.

00:37:21.670 --> 00:37:23.080
I think the answer is yes.

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:27.910
As we mentioned,
through BBSA we get to have some of these

00:37:27.910 --> 00:37:32.960
tough conversations, and we get to talk
what is it like being a black woman, and

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:37.480
we get to talk with our male counterparts
about here's some of the unique challenges

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:41.020
that we face, that maybe you guys
aren't really as in tune with.

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:44.760
I think the other thing that
Stanford has taught me is,

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:50.056
I think using my voice and
really feeling comfortable with my truth.

00:37:50.056 --> 00:37:53.896
Whether that's through talk,
whether that's through leadership labs,

00:37:53.896 --> 00:37:57.856
actually getting the feedback that I
think sometimes as an African American,

00:37:57.856 --> 00:38:01.158
getting the feedback that you're
articulate can be a little eh.

00:38:01.158 --> 00:38:01.843
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:38:01.843 --> 00:38:04.613
then I actually realized because
one of my classmates was like, no,

00:38:04.613 --> 00:38:06.110
you're actually very articulate.

00:38:06.110 --> 00:38:07.150
You are above the average.

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:08.040
And I was like, so okay, so for

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:12.610
the first time, actually realized,
okay, this is what this looks like.

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:15.850
So, I think it's really important
to recognize that you're constantly

00:38:15.850 --> 00:38:19.990
getting feedback, and through that
feedback there is that self reflection and

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:24.750
recognizing that, okay, here's how I
land on other people or present to other

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:28.140
people, and therefore here's what I
can do as a business leader to either

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:32.770
double down on that or I need to adjust
some of my behavior to seem more warm and

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:36.130
more welcoming or whatever that may be.

00:38:36.130 --> 00:38:40.426
So I don't think there's like,
there wasn't a class on how to be a black

00:38:40.426 --> 00:38:45.429
leader, but through these experiences and
recognizing and reflecting, I think,

00:38:45.429 --> 00:38:50.291
has allowed me to recognize there are
challenges, and having conversations with

00:38:50.291 --> 00:38:55.320
classmates, having the courage to speak
up, there is a way to address some things.

00:38:55.320 --> 00:39:00.630
Okay, have you recognized that this makes
me feel this way when you say this?

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:04.400
Just really having the courage to speak up
and then kind of stand in my own truth,

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:05.650
I think is important too.

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:06.818
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
There is a class though,

00:39:06.818 --> 00:39:08.310
Leadership from Diverse Perspectives.

00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:08.957
&gt;&gt; Yes, sorry.

00:39:08.957 --> 00:39:09.457
There is that class.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:09.457 --> 00:39:10.998
&gt;&gt; I'm going to it in the fall, so

00:39:10.998 --> 00:39:13.188
I am excited to get into that.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and

00:39:13.188 --> 00:39:17.622
I am building diverse organizations, so
there are some diversity related ones.

00:39:17.622 --> 00:39:18.312
Okay, so

00:39:18.312 --> 00:39:20.291
this is going to be a rapid one for
you guys.

00:39:21.330 --> 00:39:23.710
Let's talk about the academic experience.

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:25.816
What's the curriculum like,

00:39:25.816 --> 00:39:30.520
maybe high level case studies
versus experiential versus lecture.

00:39:32.550 --> 00:39:34.110
&gt;&gt; They're different.

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:37.060
There's so many different ways to
learn here, which I think are awesome.

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:41.450
So we do have classes that you read a case
each week and you kind of discuss it.

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:46.661
We have classes where each week is,
I'm taking a finance class,

00:39:46.661 --> 00:39:51.508
for example, right now and
it's awesome, I love my class.

00:39:51.508 --> 00:39:55.798
So essentially it feels like I have two
professors who really it feels like they

00:39:55.798 --> 00:39:58.490
write a script the night
before every night and-

00:39:58.490 --> 00:39:59.358
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I did that last year.

00:39:59.358 --> 00:40:00.693
They're great, yeah.
&gt;&gt; And feed off of each other for,

00:40:00.693 --> 00:40:01.318
yeah, finance.

00:40:01.318 --> 00:40:06.570
And so they helped me learn finance in
a way that I've never learned it before.

00:40:06.570 --> 00:40:09.520
It's not about just equations.

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:11.534
It's about actually
understanding why things happen.

00:40:11.534 --> 00:40:14.646
And really,
when I'm paying attention in the class,

00:40:14.646 --> 00:40:18.520
I actually can apply it to whatever
I'll be doing in the future.

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:23.260
And then they bring in professionals from
CFOs of Fortune 500 companies who we can

00:40:23.260 --> 00:40:27.260
really ask questions, deep and
meaningful and applicable questions too.

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:30.780
So I'll let you guys talk about
your experiences but there's so

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:32.260
many different ways to learn here.

00:40:32.260 --> 00:40:32.935
It's pretty awesome.

00:40:32.935 --> 00:40:35.746
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there is a core curriculum
around management foundations which,

00:40:35.746 --> 00:40:38.117
as the title says,
it's foundational courses in business.

00:40:38.117 --> 00:40:38.716
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:40:38.716 --> 00:40:43.380
&gt;&gt; But I think Ruth alluded to this
earlier, and there's classes that you've

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:45.350
taken the business school,
you can take classes across the street.

00:40:45.350 --> 00:40:49.950
One of my favorite classes was across the
street is and other programs at Stanford.

00:40:49.950 --> 00:40:51.915
One of my favorite classes was in
the engineering school called,

00:40:51.915 --> 00:40:53.150
it's called the lean launch pad.

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:57.679
It's a bunch of folks from all different
schools coming together to explore product

00:40:57.679 --> 00:41:00.433
market fit,
it's taught by a bunch of folks in BC.

00:41:00.433 --> 00:41:01.997
They bring in mentors from industry, so

00:41:01.997 --> 00:41:05.020
that was a great class
that's super experiential.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:06.810
But I mean even at the business school,

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:09.580
I'm in a class that's all about
social ventures practicum.

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:10.640
I mentioned it earlier, but

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:13.503
we essentially go through what
is needed in a business plan.

00:41:13.503 --> 00:41:15.341
And each class is focused
on one piece of that.

00:41:15.341 --> 00:41:18.070
They bring in leaders who have
actually gone through that class and

00:41:18.070 --> 00:41:19.492
are now leading social ventures.

00:41:19.492 --> 00:41:22.561
So someone came in from I think
it was Zimbabwe last week.

00:41:22.561 --> 00:41:25.622
Someone was calling in from Pakistan or,
you know what I mean?

00:41:25.622 --> 00:41:28.930
It was like a lot of different people
doing different things, which was cool.

00:41:28.930 --> 00:41:33.250
And so lots of group projects,
lots of different ways to learn.

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:35.330
And I think that's good since
everyone learns differently.

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:39.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, cool, I know there are still some

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:42.130
questions on here in career and
professional development.

00:41:42.130 --> 00:41:44.253
So I'm just going to go ahead and
take those.

00:41:44.253 --> 00:41:48.400
So there's a question on structured
mentorship programs and opportunities.

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:49.356
I'd say it's not structured.

00:41:49.356 --> 00:41:53.255
There are some things that we have
with the career management center.

00:41:53.255 --> 00:41:56.737
But the career management center,
I also sit on that committee,

00:41:56.737 --> 00:42:01.170
has programs available to you if you're
trying to transition from social sector to

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:04.010
consulting, there's a consulting club.

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:05.990
We also have a finance and
investments club.

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:09.619
We have various clubs that take on
essentially the mentorship role.

00:42:09.619 --> 00:42:13.323
And then when you start at Stanford
you'll have a fellow, an Arbuckle fellow,

00:42:13.323 --> 00:42:16.418
who's a second year who's also
there to help coach you as well, so

00:42:16.418 --> 00:42:17.654
that's an opportunity.

00:42:21.270 --> 00:42:22.750
Question for the president.

00:42:22.750 --> 00:42:24.327
What's next for you after the GSV?

00:42:24.327 --> 00:42:25.128
I'll take that.

00:42:25.128 --> 00:42:26.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So for

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:31.950
me, I will be moving to Chicago and
working at the Boston Consulting Group.

00:42:31.950 --> 00:42:35.180
So I get a nice little break, so
I'll probably do some traveling and

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:36.330
hanging out with folks.

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:39.920
Okay, so last question for everyone.

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:46.560
Did you pay a visit to campus before
applying and if so was the visit worth it?

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:51.829
And then when would you recommend
someone visit and yeah.

00:42:51.829 --> 00:42:52.520
&gt;&gt; Ooh.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:57.180
&gt;&gt; So like I said, my application
process was a little bit different.

00:42:57.180 --> 00:42:58.601
I didn't know a ton of
people before applying.

00:42:58.601 --> 00:43:04.660
I did visit Stanford but I didn't even
tell administration what my name was.

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:09.261
Because at that point, I didn't feel
like saying who I was, was going to move

00:43:09.261 --> 00:43:13.560
the needle and I think that that
played out as I thought it would.

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:18.700
I think visiting the Stanford campus,
while it is important, I do think that

00:43:18.700 --> 00:43:23.040
you should have a reason as to why you're
visiting if you really need to be here.

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:25.458
I do think the pictures
do it great justice and

00:43:25.458 --> 00:43:27.505
California's what people say it is.

00:43:27.505 --> 00:43:29.739
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So if you are for example,

00:43:29.739 --> 00:43:33.674
applying from a different country,
don't feel like you're at a loss

00:43:33.674 --> 00:43:37.620
because you can't be physically
on the campus before you apply.

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:41.640
But I will definitely say that if you
apply and you do get in, definitely,

00:43:41.640 --> 00:43:42.960
obviously, visit the campus for

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:47.680
admit weekend just get to see how
the vibe is before you actually come.

00:43:47.680 --> 00:43:49.850
&gt;&gt; Is there another
time they should visit?

00:43:49.850 --> 00:43:52.751
&gt;&gt; So
my application process was very long.

00:43:52.751 --> 00:43:55.344
I most recently was in
the Bay Area before school but

00:43:55.344 --> 00:43:57.320
also was in New York before that.

00:43:57.320 --> 00:43:59.450
And so
I visited campus when I was in New York.

00:43:59.450 --> 00:44:03.278
I came for the diverse like Perspectives
Day which I think it's coming up on for

00:44:03.278 --> 00:44:06.359
black students,
historically underrepresented students.

00:44:06.359 --> 00:44:07.870
And that was actually
a really great event.

00:44:07.870 --> 00:44:11.412
I loved meeting other folks who were
applying, hearing different backgrounds.

00:44:11.412 --> 00:44:16.359
I also love the alumni who came back and
talked about their experience at the GSP,

00:44:16.359 --> 00:44:17.833
met admissions folks.

00:44:17.833 --> 00:44:18.991
That was helpful for

00:44:18.991 --> 00:44:22.338
me in kind of building this in
as what's Stanford would be.

00:44:22.338 --> 00:44:25.327
And then when I lived in the Bay,
it was easy to come down and

00:44:25.327 --> 00:44:27.200
check out campus again.

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:29.340
&gt;&gt; I also visited, sorry to interrupt you.

00:44:29.340 --> 00:44:33.864
But we had the, when I did get in,
I came for admin weekend which was also

00:44:33.864 --> 00:44:39.010
the conference weekend which we're
holding this year on May 10th and 11th.

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:39.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:44.772
&gt;&gt; And so, it was an amazing experience
for me because I think one of the keynote

00:44:44.772 --> 00:44:50.133
speakers that tday was the person who
heads up all of Beyonce's conglomerate.

00:44:50.133 --> 00:44:50.772
&gt;&gt; Casual.

00:44:50.772 --> 00:44:55.021
[CROSSTALK] [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So it was such a great experience to

00:44:55.021 --> 00:44:59.638
meet so many amazing professionals,
so many amazing alumni from decades

00:44:59.638 --> 00:45:03.890
to recently, so many amazing
prospectives and current students.

00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:06.100
So that was a really great weekend for me.

00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:07.700
That's where I also met Fometta.

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:10.370
I also met a lot of people
who are my classmates now.

00:45:10.370 --> 00:45:11.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so quick plug for that.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:16.220
If you go to BBSA Conference.com,
you can find all the information.

00:45:16.220 --> 00:45:21.300
There also will be perspective
student's day portion on the 10th.

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:24.020
So look for
all that information on our website.

00:45:24.020 --> 00:45:27.130
And also as mentioned,
Diversity Day will be on the 18th and

00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:30.250
we'll also have kind of
a deep dive with professors.

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:34.458
And you'll probably see us and
many more of the current students as well.

00:45:34.458 --> 00:45:36.634
All right, so we're out of time.

00:45:36.634 --> 00:45:40.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so I would love you

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:46.090
to give you guys ten seconds before
we go to say any final words,

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:50.150
advice for people thinking and
applying this in for an MBA.

00:45:50.150 --> 00:45:54.434
&gt;&gt; I would say that Standford is and
can be for you whatever your passions are,

00:45:54.434 --> 00:45:58.175
whatever you aspire to,
you can come here and make that reality.

00:45:58.175 --> 00:46:02.598
It's a beautiful place where I'd never
felt like I could do whatever I wanted

00:46:02.598 --> 00:46:04.310
to until I came to Standford.

00:46:04.310 --> 00:46:07.760
And I think it's partially because of
the community, because of the resources.

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:10.469
But I hope to see you here on campus soon.

00:46:10.469 --> 00:46:14.508
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would definitely say Stanford
is a one of a kind place where you get

00:46:14.508 --> 00:46:17.453
an experience that can only
be the best thing for you.

00:46:17.453 --> 00:46:20.577
And I would definitely say that
in terms of relationships not

00:46:20.577 --> 00:46:24.510
just with students but with professors,
with companies in the area.

00:46:24.510 --> 00:46:27.210
It's definitely been a great
value add in my life so

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:32.060
I would encourage you guys to apply and
yes, hopefully see you guys next fall.

00:46:32.060 --> 00:46:33.507
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
you won't see me because I will be gone.

00:46:33.507 --> 00:46:35.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Fall after that [CROSSTALK]

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:36.656
&gt;&gt; Fall after that yeah, I'm so

00:46:36.656 --> 00:46:38.139
tired when I come back to campus
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:46:38.139 --> 00:46:40.474
&gt;&gt; But anyway, great, thank you both, and

00:46:40.474 --> 00:46:44.299
thank you guys for joining us today and
your thoughtful questions.

00:46:44.299 --> 00:46:47.551
Again, we encourage you to reach out.

00:46:47.551 --> 00:46:51.174
Admissions can put you in contact with
anybody in the BBSA if you do have any

00:46:51.174 --> 00:46:53.638
more specific questions
that we didn't answer.

00:46:53.638 --> 00:46:56.591
And we look forward to seeing you for
the conference or

00:46:56.591 --> 00:46:59.749
Diversity Day,
the weekend of the 10th or the 18th.

00:46:59.749 --> 00:47:00.464
Thank you.

00:47:00.464 --> 00:47:01.093
&gt;&gt; Thank you, guys.

